for (uintnat i = 0, pos = pos_initial(t, key);
     i < MAX_CHAIN;
     i++, pos = pos_next(t, pos)) {
    if (t->entries[pos].key == ADDRMAP_INVALID_KEY) break;
    if (t->entries[pos].key == key) return 1;
}
```

The difference: in `contains`, when an empty slot is hit, the search stops (break). That is correct for open addressing: the item can only be in the first empty slot or before. In `insert_pos`, when it finds an empty slot it fills in the key, but then continues to look for a pre-existing entry with that key. That continuation is wrong. It can very well lead to a pre-existing entry being found in a slot beyond the first empty one, and the new one being returned in that place. The result is that the same thing is to be found in the table under two different names. When the operation that has to do with the passing from one of the things to another is to make use of such a table, the thing to which the direction is made to point is to be found in a place in which in no way to be expected to be. That is to produce in the result the like failure that has been observed on all occasions.

The thing that has to be done is to put the one that acts in that wrong manner into a form in which the trying is to be terminated at the same point as in the other. In the event of that being effected the whole matter is on all grounds to be looked for to be in a condition to be brought to a satisfactory end. There is very little to be left to conjecture in the matter after that, the whole having been in the inquiries already made reduced to so simple a condition.

